<h3 id="checked">Checked</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Each figure and quotation was taken from the source named beside it, on the date shown. The reading was done with an AI assistant, which opened the source and copied the figure or passage.</li>
  <li>The two headline rates were checked a second time, directly against GOV.UK.</li>
  <li>The calculators were tested against examples worked out separately, by a different method from the calculator code.</li>
  <li>Every increase in the published rates from 2011/12, and in the new State Pension from 2017/18, was tested against the rate before it, the increase applied, and rounding to the nearest 5p.</li>
  <li>The owner read every page on {{ ownerReadDate | safe }} and gave notes page by page, and the AI assistant made a readability review of every page the same day.</li>
  {% if site.reviewerName and site.reviewDate %}<li>The content was reviewed by {{ site.reviewerName }} on {{ site.reviewDate | timeTag | safe }}.</li>{% endif %}
  <li>The four tools are driven in a browser by a script: <a href="#how-the-calculators-were-tested">how the calculators were tested</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="not-checked">Not checked</h3>
<ul>
  {% if not (site.reviewerName and site.reviewDate) %}<li><strong>The owner's second read of every page, after the changes made since {{ ownerReadDate | safe }}, is not finished.</strong> When it is finished, its date is given under Checked. A check by the same assistant that wrote a page is not an independent review.</li>{% endif %}
  <li>Some sources refused automated reading, including the House of Commons Library and Hansard. Nothing on this site is quoted from them.</li>
  <li>Figures reported in the news without a document we could open are left out, or are marked as reported.</li>
  <li>The accessibility assessment is set out in the <a href="/accessibility/">accessibility statement</a>.</li>
</ul>

<p>We do not use labels such as "verified" or "official" as a general mark of reliability. Each figure says what it is, where it is from and when it was checked.</p>

<h2 id="approach">Editorial approach</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Neutral.</strong> The site explains. It does not recommend a policy. The site puts the same questions to every option, including keeping the present rules.</li>
  <li><strong>We do not treat evidence as equal when it is not.</strong> We state a claim with strong evidence plainly. A claim with weak or old evidence says so.</li>
  <li><strong>We keep findings and judgements apart.</strong> We name a research body's recommendation as that body's recommendation.</li>
  <li><strong>We show gaps.</strong> Where we did not find a figure, the page says so. We do not fill a gap with an estimate of our own or with an example household.</li>
  <li><strong>When sources disagree, we show both.</strong> We say how they differ. We do not pick one without saying.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="source-order">Which sources we use, and in what order</h2>

<ol>
  <li><strong>The law and official rates.</strong> Acts, orders and the rates published by the Department for Work and Pensions. Used for rules and rates.</li>
  <li><strong>Official statistics.</strong> The Department for Work and Pensions, the Office for National Statistics and HM Revenue and Customs. Used for what is observed. We note where statistics are in development or where accreditation is suspended.</li>
  <li><strong>Official forecasts and projections.</strong> The Office for Budget Responsibility and the Government Actuary's Department. Always shown with their edition and assumptions.</li>
  <li><strong>Research bodies.</strong> For analysis and benchmarks, named each time. Their recommendations are not official rules.</li>
  <li><strong>News reports.</strong> Only for what has been reported or said. Never as the only authority for a rule or a costing.</li>
</ol>

<p>Our sister sites are not used as evidence for any claim here. Links to them are for further explanation.</p>

<h2 id="dates">What the dates on this site mean</h2>

<dl>
  <dt>Period</dt> <dd>The time a figure describes, such as a tax year.</dd>
  <dt>Took effect</dt> <dd>The date a rate or rule started to apply.</dd>
  <dt>Published</dt> <dd>The date the source was published, where the source gives one.</dd>
  <dt>Checked against the source</dt> <dd>The date we read the source. Checking a source again does not change a page's content date.</dd>
  <dt>Content last changed</dt> <dd>The date the content of a page last changed in substance. It does not change when the site is rebuilt.</dd>
</dl>

<h2 id="models">How the calculators work, and their limits</h2>

<h3 id="what-all-four-have-in-common">What all four have in common</h3>
<ul>
  <li>They work in your browser. The history explorer puts its settings in the page address when it shows a result. The rule comparison and the triple lock calculator put nothing there on their own: each has a button that copies a link to the result shown. Nothing else you enter is sent or stored. <a href="/privacy/#tools">Privacy</a>.</li>
  <li>Money is held in whole pence and percentages in tenths of a per cent, so no rounding happens except where the method says.</li>
  <li>The three calculators about pension rates work before tax, on a full rate of one pension, not on a person's whole payment.</li>
  <li>The budget explorer takes a household's whole income and has a line for Income Tax that the reader fills in.</li>
  <li>None works out tax, Pension Credit or any other benefit.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="the-five-rules">The five rules</h3>
{% call m.scrollTable() %}
<table>
  <caption id="table-rule-increases">The increase each rule applies</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Rule</th><th scope="col">Increase applied</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Triple lock</th><td>The highest of prices, earnings and 2.5%</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Double lock</th><td>The higher of prices and earnings</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Earnings link</th><td>Earnings</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Prices link</th><td>Prices</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Rule announced for April 2030</th><td>The highest of prices, 2.5%, and the increase needed to bring the rate up to the starting amount carried forward by earnings growth. Our reading of the Institute for Fiscal Studies' description, with four assumptions. <a href="/reform-options/compare/#rule-2030-assumptions">The assumptions</a></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
{% endcall %}

<dl>
  <dt>Ties</dt> <dd>When two or three measures are equal highest, the result names each of them.</dd>
  <dt>Falling prices or earnings</dt> <dd>The triple lock and the rule announced for April 2030 always give at least 2.5%. For the other rules, a negative figure becomes 0% when the cash floor is on. The cash floor is an assumption of these hypothetical rules, not a statement of the law. It can be switched off in the rule comparison.</dd>
  <dt>Rounding</dt> <dd>The new weekly amount is rounded to the nearest 5p, and an exact half rounds up. The rounded amount is the base for the next year. {{ m.c("rounding-5p") }}</dd>
  <dt>A year</dt> <dd>52 weeks at one rate. This is an equivalent. It is not what a person receives in a tax year.</dd>
  <dt>Timing</dt> <dd>The rule comparison does not model the delay between the figures and the April increase. The history explorer uses the figures recorded for each decision.</dd>
</dl>

<h3 id="what-the-calculators-leave-out">What the calculators leave out</h3>
<ul>
  <li>A freeze, and a smoothed earnings link. <a href="/reform-options/#smoothed">Why the smoothed link is not in the calculator</a>.</li>
  <li>Any estimate of national cost or saving.</li>
  <li>Any link between a future pension and a household budget. That would need future prices, tax and benefits, which the tools do not have.</li>
  <li>Probabilities. The scenarios are illustrations.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="how-the-calculators-were-tested">How the calculators were tested</h3>
<p>The expected results were worked out in a separate program that uses decimal arithmetic, and by hand, before being compared with the calculators. The tests cover ties, zero, negative figures, an exact half in rounding, each of the five rules, the cash floor on and off, each way of entering a budget, and the exceptional years in the history. A separate method of arithmetic is a check on the code. It is not a review by another person. A second script drives the four tools in a browser as a reader would, and checks what each shows and announces after a change and a wrong entry, and, where a tool has them, after a copied link and a malformed address.</p>

<h2 id="limits">Known limits and likely misreadings</h2>

<p>Each of these is said where it applies. They are gathered here so that a reader can see them in one place.</p>

<h3 id="limits-known">Known limits</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The rule announced for April 2030 is not law, and its detail is thin.</strong> The government has described it in outline. It has not published the measure of earnings it would use, or the paths of earnings and prices behind its savings. The rule comparison follows the Institute for Fiscal Studies' description, with <a href="/reform-options/compare/#rule-2030-assumptions">four assumptions</a>. <a href="/reform-options/#change-announced">What is announced</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>The government's savings figures can be read but not reproduced.</strong> The Department for Work and Pensions gives the baseline, the coverage and the prices, and not what it assumed about earnings and prices. <a href="/reform-options/costs/#saving">The saving from a change</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Some responses to the announcement of 29 September 2026 rest on news reports.</strong> The main parties' and organisations' statements come from their own sites where we found them; the rest are as reported, and say so. <a href="/reform-options/social-care/#status-reported">Reported, not confirmed in a government document</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>The example budgets are not like for like.</strong> Private renters are at the 2026 minimum standard, social renters at the 2025 minimum, and owners at the 2025 moderate standard. <a href="/tools/budget/#budget">The budget explorer</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Some figures were not found, and the pages say so:</strong> <a href="/state-pension/#not-available-the-share-of-people-on-the-full-rate">the share of people on the full rate</a>, <a href="/state-pension/#not-available-pension-not-increased">the number whose pension is not increased abroad</a>, <a href="/reform-options/costs/#not-available-costings-double-lock-means-testing">costings for a double lock and for means-testing</a>, and <a href="/reform-options/costs/#not-available-some-breakdowns">the incomes of pensioner carers</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Whether a person has reviewed the content, and how far the accessibility assessment has gone,</strong> are under <a href="#review-status">what has and has not been checked</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="limits-misreadings">Likely misreadings</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A full rate is not everyone's payment.</strong> Many people get less and some get more. <a href="/state-pension/#what-people-receive">What people receive</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>A lower amount under another rule is a smaller rise, not a cut,</strong> unless the amount falls in pounds, which the cash floor prevents when it is on. <a href="/reform-options/#two-decisions">Two different decisions</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>The calculators' results are illustrations.</strong> They are not forecasts, and not anyone's entitlement. <a href="#models">How the calculators work</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>What the triple lock has cost is not what changing it would save.</strong> The whole bill, the cost so far and the saving from a change are three different amounts. <a href="/reform-options/costs/#cost-and-saving">The cost and the saving</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>State Pension spending is not all spending on older people.</strong> <a href="/reform-options/costs/#state-pension-spending-is-not-all-spending-on-older-people">What it leaves out</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Income, wealth and need are three different things,</strong> and an average pensioner does not exist. <a href="/reform-options/costs/#who">Who receives what</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>A research budget is not an entitlement,</strong> and falling below one is not poverty as official statistics measure it. <a href="/living-on-a-pension/#three-questions">Three questions about living costs</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>A 4-week payment is not a month's money.</strong> <a href="/state-pension/#a-4-week-payment-is-not-a-month-s-money">How it is paid</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Figures on different price bases must not be added or compared with each other.</strong> <a href="/reform-options/social-care/#care-scale">The sums involved</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>A pension payment and a care service are different things,</strong> and the two policies cannot yet be treated as covering the same people. <a href="/reform-options/social-care/#care-two-things">Why they differ</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="ai">Use of AI</h2>

<p>This site was researched, written and built with an AI assistant, Claude, made by Anthropic, working under the direction of the site's owner. The assistant read the sources, drafted the text, wrote the code and ran the tests. {% if site.reviewerName and site.reviewDate %}{{ site.reviewerName }} reviewed the content on {{ site.reviewDate | timeTag | safe }}.{% else %}The owner read every page on {{ ownerReadDate | safe }}; the second read, after the changes made since, is not finished.{% endif %}</p>

<p>An AI assistant can misread a source or state something wrongly with confidence. That is why each figure shows its source, and the passage it rests on where one is quoted, so that a reader can check it.</p>

